realloc returns the SAME block when the one it has is already big enough.

WHAT THIS IS TESTING. Patch 0011's third round ends with a named hypothesis
and no way to choose between it and its alternative:

    "a pointer from section_ptr_add held across a later section_realloc would
     write through a stale base, which is the classic shape"

The reason to suspect that HERE and not in tcc generally is an asymmetry
between this allocator and glibc's, and it is total:

    glibc realloc     grows IN PLACE whenever the chunk has room, which for a
                      doubling growth pattern is most of the time
    M2libc realloc    ALWAYS allocates a new block, copies, and frees the old
                      -- unconditionally, on every call, even a SHRINK

So a caller holding a pointer across a realloc is correct-by-luck under glibc
and wrong on every single call under M2libc. That is the difference between
the gcc-built control, which passes tcc's whole suite, and mc-tcc, which
corrupts its heap -- from the SAME SOURCE.

WHY THIS IS A FIX AND NOT A MASK. M2libc rounds every allocation up to a power
of two at or above 256:

    size_t used = 256;
    while(used < size) used = used << 1;

so a node's block is nearly always larger than what was asked for, and a
`realloc` growing from 300 to 400 bytes is asking for room the block ALREADY
HAS. Returning it is what every production allocator does, it is permitted by
ISO C, and it is what the node's own `size` field is for. The current code
allocates 512 bytes, copies 300, and frees 512 -- for nothing. tcc's
`tal_realloc_impl` and `cstr_realloc` both grow by doubling, so every CString
and every section in a compile currently pays a full copy per growth.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not shrink the block. Nothing here needs the
memory back, and a shrink that recorded a smaller size would then have to
handle a later grow across the old boundary -- more bookkeeping than this
allocator has anywhere else.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIRST OUTING, AND WHAT IT ANSWERED.

Landed alone against an otherwise identical tree -- micro-c byte-identical at
`30b8f25f9ddc00b8`, the same microc.c, only mc-tcc changed, 1575057 -> 1575389
bytes. So everything below is attributable to this patch and nothing else.

    18_include        UNCHANGED -- still "free: pointer was never returned"
    33_ternary_op     REGRESSED -- compiled in the two previous runs, now
                      fails at line 37 with "struct or union expected"

THE FIRST HALF IS THE ANSWER THE PATCH WAS LANDED FOR, and it is a negative
one: **a pointer held across a realloc is ruled out.** That class cannot fire
any more -- a growth inside the block's headroom no longer moves anything --
and the corruption is unchanged. 0011's closing hypothesis is wrong, and the
node loss is caused by something else.

THE SECOND HALF IS WORTH MORE THAN IT LOOKS. 33_ternary_op:37 is

    int i1 = (i != 0 ? 0 : s)->i;

a conditional whose arms are a null pointer constant and a struct pointer.
tcctest.c:4082 is the SAME CONSTRUCT and has failed with the SAME MESSAGE in
every run this spike has recorded, back to before any of this. So the
corruption is not landing randomly: it damages whatever backs that
construct's type resolution, and moving the heap moved it onto a second
witness of the same thing. Two witnesses of one fault is a better position
than one, and tcctest.c is the gate step 12 is waiting on.

WHAT CHANGED HERE SINCE THAT RUN. The comparison is done at ONE WIDTH.
`size` is realloc's `unsigned` parameter -- four bytes -- and `have->size` is
a size_t; comparing them directly is a mixed-width comparison, and this
compiler has a recorded history of getting exactly those wrong
(EXPERIMENT-zzzc, -zzza, -zzzw). Backwards, it would hand back a block TOO
SMALL, and the caller would write past its end into the next node's `next`
field -- which is precisely the corruption shape 0011 measured. That would
make this patch a CAUSE rather than a probe. One `size_t want = size;`
removes the possibility; if 33_ternary_op comes back with it, the mixed-width
comparison was the regression and the patch stands. If 33 still fails, the
patch is a perturbation of a fault that is upstream of the allocator either
way, and the thing to chase is the ternary type path both witnesses share.

